  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The aniseed cockleshell (Lentinellus cochleatus) is a small, delicate fungus with a distinctive shell or fan-like shape and a pleasant anise-like aroma. This edible species belongs to the Auriscalpiaceae family and is commonly found decomposing woody debris throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Shell-shaped cap",
      "description": "Distinctive small, thin, cockleshell or fan-shaped fruiting bodies, typically 1-3 cm wide, with a wavy or scalloped margin",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Anise scent",
      "description": "Characteristic sweet aniseed or licorice-like smell, especially when fresh or crushed",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Decurrent gills",
      "description": "Small, crowded gills that extend down the stem and are white to pale cream in color",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species grows on hardwood logs, stumps, and dead branches in moist, shaded forests throughout New England, typically appearing from late summer through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its delicate appearance and pleasant licorice aroma, the aniseed cockleshell must be properly prepared before consumption, as raw specimens can cause digestive upset in some people.",
